          "title": "Theory of Multi-Radix Positional Numerals in Iris Arithmetic",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "== Theory of Multi-Radix Positional Numerals in Iris Arithmetic\n\nIn this section, positional numeral systems across arbitrary discrete bases (radices) \\( b \\ge 2 \\) are formally established as exact multivector aperture expansions in \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\). We demonstrate that digit strings in binary (base 2), octal (base 8), decimal (base 10), and hexadecimal (base 16) represent invariant discrete scalar counting states under Main Scale Projection \\( ( \\downarrow ) \\).\n\n[#theorem-radix-representation]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Multi-Radix Representation and Uniqueness Theorem\n====\nFor any discrete radix integer count \\( b \\ge 2 \\) and any positive measurement step count \\( n \\in \\mathbb { Z } ^ + \\), there exists a unique finite sequence of discrete digit coefficients \\( ( d _ M , d _ { M - 1 } , \\dots , d _ 1 , d _ 0 ) \\) with \\( d _ k \\in \\{ 0 , 1 , \\dots , b - 1 \\} \\) and \\( d _ M \\neq 0 \\) such that: \\[ M ( n ) = n \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } = \\sum _ { k = 0 } ^ M d _ k b ^ k \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\]\n\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Discrete Division Algorithm Iteration**: On partition grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\), apply the Euclidean division step recursively starting with \\( n _ 0 = n \\): \\[ n _ k = q _ k b + d _ k , \\quad \\text { where } 0 \\le d _ k < b \\text { and } q _ k = \\lfloor n _ k / b \\rfloor \\]\n\n\n. **Strict Step Contraction**: Since \\( b \\ge 2 \\), the quotient sequence \\( q _ 0 > q _ 1 > q _ 2 > \\dots \\) decreases strictly in discrete step count, terminating at \\( q _ M = 0 \\) in a finite number of steps \\( M = \\lfloor \\log _ b n \\rfloor \\).\n\n. **Algebraic Substitution**: Substituting the remainder steps recursively yields the exact grade-0 multivector sum \\( M ( n ) = \\sum _ { k = 0 } ^ M d _ k b ^ k \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\).\n\n. **Uniqueness of Digit Sequence**: Suppose \\( \\sum _ { k = 0 } ^ M d _ k b ^ k = \\sum _ { k = 0 } ^ M c _ k b ^ k \\). Taking congruences modulo \\( b \\) forces \\( d _ 0 \\equiv c _ 0 \\pmod b \\). Since \\( 0 \\le d _ 0 , c _ 0 < b \\), \\( d _ 0 = c _ 0 \\). Subtracting \\( d _ 0 \\) and dividing by \\( b \\) yields \\( d _ 1 = c _ 1 \\), and by finite induction, \\( d _ k = c _ k \\) for all \\( k = 0 , \\dots , M \\).\n\n. **Tautological Conclusion**: Every discrete integer step count possesses a unique digit expansion in radix \\( b \\). \\( \\square \\)\n====\n\n[#theorem-radix-conversion-invariance]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Radix Transformation Invariance Theorem\n====\nLet \\( ( d _ M \\dots d _ 0 ) _ { b _ 1 } \\) and \\( ( c _ K \\dots c _ 0 ) _ { b _ 2 } \\) be numeral representations of measurement state \\( M ( n ) \\) in distinct radices \\( b _ 1 , b _ 2 \\ge 2 \\). The total multivector scalar energy and \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\) grade decomposition remain strictly invariant across radix transformations: \\[ ( \\downarrow ) \\sum _ { k = 0 } ^ M d _ k b _ 1 ^ k \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } = ( \\downarrow ) \\sum _ { j = 0 } ^ K c _ j b _ 2 ^ j \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } = n \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\]\n\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Scalar Metric Embedding**: Both positional sums evaluate to the same Grade-0 scalar multivector element \\( M ( n ) = n \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\in Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\).\n\n. **Basis Identity**: The choice of radix base \\( b \\) alters only the discrete aperture grouping mechanism, leaving the fundamental count of unit steps \\( \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\) invariant.\n\n. **Main Scale Projection**: Applying Main Scale Projection \\( ( \\downarrow ) \\) removes any intermediate radix conversion vernier residuals, proving exact operational equivalence. \\( \\square \\)\n===="
