          "id": "sec-skeleton-affine-projection-and-optimal-fat-line-bounding",
          "title": "Skeleton Affine Projection and Optimal Fat-Line Bounding",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "== Skeleton Affine Projection and Optimal Fat-Line Bounding\n\nThis section establishes coordinate transformations mapping curve skeletons to standard orientation and derives the optimal parallel fat-line enclosure in the \\( s \\)-power basis.\n\n[#theorem-optimal-fat-line-width]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Optimal s-Power Fat-Line Bounding and Skeleton Projection\n====\nLet \\( P ( t ) \\) be a clipper curve with non-coincident endpoints \\( P _ 0 , P _ 2 \\). The affine projection \\( \\mathbf { T } _ { \\text { skel } } \\) mapping \\( P _ 0 \\mapsto ( 0 , 0 ) \\) and \\( P _ 2 \\mapsto ( 1 , 0 ) \\) transforms \\( P ( t ) \\) into projected curve \\( P ' ( t ) = ( t + ( 1 - t ) t d _ x , ( 1 - t ) t d _ y ) \\), where \\( ( d _ x , d _ y ) = \\mathbf { T } _ { \\text { skel } } ( P _ 1 ^ { \\Delta } ) \\).\n\nThe exact minimum parallel fat-line bounds enclosing \\( P ' ( t ) \\) for all \\( t \\in [ 0 , 1 ] \\) are given by \\( y = d _ { \\text { min } } \\) and \\( y = d _ { \\text { max } } \\), where:\n\\[ d _ { \\text { min } } = \\min \\left ( 0 , \\frac { d _ y } { 4 } \\right ) - \\epsilon _ { \\text { pad } } , \\quad d _ { \\text { max } } = \\max \\left ( 0 , \\frac { d _ y } { 4 } \\right ) + \\epsilon _ { \\text { pad } } \\]\nThe central coefficient \\( d _ y / 4 \\) is exactly four times the signed optimal fat-line width, providing a tighter bound than control polygon convex hulls without generating Bernstein control points.\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Skeleton Coordinate Matrix**: The affine transformation matrix mapping segment \\( P _ 0 P _ 2 \\) to \\( ( 0 , 0 ) \\to ( 1 , 0 ) \\) is constructed by defining vector \\( \\mathbf { v } = P _ 2 - P _ 0 = ( \\Delta x , \\Delta y ) \\). With norm squared \\( L ^ 2 = \\Delta x ^ 2 + \\Delta y ^ 2 \\neq 0 \\), the transformation maps point \\( Q \\) to \\( Q ' \\):\n\\[ x ' = \\frac { \\Delta x ( x _ Q - x _ 0 ) + \\Delta y ( y _ Q - y _ 0 ) } { L ^ 2 } , \\quad y ' = \\frac { - \\Delta y ( x _ Q - x _ 0 ) + \\Delta x ( y _ Q - y _ 0 ) } { L ^ 2 } \\]\nUnder this projection, \\( P _ 0 ' = ( 0 , 0 ) \\) and \\( P _ 2 ' = ( 1 , 0 ) \\).\n\n. **Y-Coordinate Extreme Value**: In projected coordinates, the distance function to the skeleton line is simply the y-coordinate \\( y ' ( t ) = ( 1 - t ) t d _ y \\). Differentiation yields \\( \\frac { d y ' } { d t } = ( 1 - 2 t ) d _ y \\). Setting the derivative to zero gives the unique critical point at \\( t = 1 / 2 \\).\n\n. **Optimal Bound Value**: Evaluating at \\( t = 1 / 2 \\) gives \\( y ' ( 1 / 2 ) = \\left ( 1 - \\frac { 1 } { 2 } \\right ) \\frac { 1 } { 2 } d _ y = \\frac { d _ y } { 4 } \\). Since \\( ( 1 - t ) t \\ge 0 \\) for all \\( t \\in [ 0 , 1 ] \\), the y-coordinate is bounded strictly between \\( 0 \\) and \\( d _ y / 4 \\). Adding numerical padding \\( \\epsilon _ { \\text { pad } } \\) ensures strict containment on finite rational grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\). \\( \\square \\)\n===="
