          "id": "sec-numerical-stability-safeguards-for-computational-topology-in-engineering",
          "title": "Numerical Stability Safeguards for Computational Topology in Engineering",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Numerical Stability Safeguards for Computational Topology in Engineering\n\nWhen evaluating topological invariants, helicity sums, and defect indices on discrete rational grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\), the following practical engineering recommendations are provided:\n\n1. **Defect Core Buffer Separation**:\n   - Loop contours \\( \\Gamma \\) used for winding index calculation must maintain a minimum buffer distance \\( d ( \\Gamma , \\text { Core } ) \\ge 2 \\delta \\) from singular defect centers where \\( | \\mathbf { \\psi } | < \\epsilon _ { \\text { core } } = 2 ^ { - F / 2 } \\), preventing phase ambiguity from zero-vector normalization.\n\n2. **Discrete Gauge Fixing and Divergence Cleansing**:\n   - For plasma magnetic fields and fluid velocity fields, numerical rounding can introduce artificial solenoidal errors \\( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf { B } \\neq 0 \\). Apply discrete Hodge-Helmholtz projection:\n     \\[ \\mathbf { B } _ { \\text { clean } } = \\mathbf { B } - \\nabla \\left( \\nabla ^ 2 \\right) ^ { - 1 } ( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf { B } ) \\]\n     at each time step to preserve exact topological flux conservation.\n\n[#example-practical-plasma-topology]\n[EXAMPLE]\n.Example Problem 12: Topological Helicity and Defect Index Calculation in Tokamak Plasma Vortex\n====\n*Problem*: Calculate the discrete magnetic helicity \\( H _ { \\mathcal { I } } \\) and winding index \\( n \\) for two linked magnetic flux loops in a discrete tokamak plasma grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) obeying Newton's dynamics. Loop 1 carries magnetic flux \\( \\Phi_1 = 3 \\) Weber along circle \\( C_1 \\), and Loop 2 carries magnetic flux \\( \\Phi_2 = 2 \\) Weber along circle \\( C_2 \\) linked once with \\( C_1 \\).\n\n*Solution*:\n\n1. **Gauss Linking Topology**:\n   - Pairwise linking number between flux loops \\( C_1 \\) and \\( C_2 \\) is \\( L k ( C_1 , C_2 ) = 1 \\).\n\n2. **Discrete Magnetic Helicity Evaluation**:\n   - According to the Discrete Woltjer-Taylor Helicity Theorem, the magnetic helicity of linked thin flux tubes simplifies to:\n     \\[ H _ { \\mathcal { I } } = 2 \\cdot L k ( C_1 , C_2 ) \\cdot \\Phi_1 \\cdot \\Phi_2 \\]\n   - Substituting values:\n     \\[ H _ { \\mathcal { I } } = 2 \\cdot ( 1 ) \\cdot ( 3 ) \\cdot ( 2 ) = 12 \\text{ Weber} ^ 2 \\]\n\n3. **Winding Index Around Defect Core**:\n   - Evaluating circulation integral along closed loop \\( \\Gamma \\) enclosing the core of Loop 1:\n     \\[ n = \\frac { 1 } { 2 \\pi } \\oint _ { \\Gamma } d \\theta = \\frac { 2 \\pi } { 2 \\pi } = 1 \\]\n   - The topological winding index \\( n = 1 \\) confirms the existence of a single, stable plasma vortex line core, demonstrating exact topological classification without continuum limit assumptions.\n===="
