          "id": "sec-dedekind-jordan-chain-condition-for-finite-modular-lattices",
          "title": "Dedekind-Jordan Chain Condition for Finite Modular Lattices",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "== Dedekind-Jordan Chain Condition for Finite Modular Lattices\n\n[#theorem-dedekind-jordan-chain]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Dedekind-Jordan Chain Condition for Finite Modular Lattices\n====\nLet \\( L \\subseteq \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) be a finite modular lattice. For any pair of elements \\( a , b \\in L \\) with \\( a \\le b \\), all maximal chains connecting \\( a \\) to \\( b \\):\n\\[ C = \\{ a = x _ 0 < x _ 1 < \\dots < x _ k = b \\} \\]\nhave the exact same finite length \\( k \\). Consequently, every finite modular lattice admits a strictly additive height function \\( h : L \\to \\mathbb { Z } _ { \\ge 0 } \\) satisfying for all \\( x , y \\in L \\):\n\\[ h ( x \\vee y ) + h ( x \\wedge y ) = h ( x ) + h ( y ) \\]\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Isomorphism of Covered Intervals**: In any modular lattice, if \\( x \\wedge y \\lessdot x \\) (where \\( \\lessdot \\) denotes the covering relation), then \\( y \\lessdot x \\vee y \\). The map \\( \\theta ( z ) = z \\vee y \\) defines an isomorphism between interval \\( [ x \\wedge y , x ] \\) and interval \\( [ y , x \\vee y ] \\).\n\n. **Induction on Height**: By induction on the length of maximal chains in interval \\( [ a , b ] \\), replacing any element in a maximal chain with a modular join/meet preserves step count.\n\n. **Additive Rank Formula**: Define \\( h ( x ) \\) as the length of any maximal chain from bottom element \\( \\mathbf { 0 } _ L \\) to \\( x \\). The modular law guarantees \\( h ( x \\vee y ) + h ( x \\wedge y ) = h ( x ) + h ( y ) \\). \\( \\square \\)\n====\n\n[#references-dedekind-jordan]"
